      "title": "Air cools as it evaporates the droplets",
      "description": "Hot inlet air (often ~ 150–250 °C, higher for robust products) meets the spray at the top. Evaporation is very fast because the droplets are tiny (large surface area); the evaporating droplets stay near the wet-bulb temperature, so even heat-sensitive products (milk, enzymes) survive. Outlet air (~ 70–100 °C) sets the final moisture.",
